WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.440
Your snacks, the science of baking, and food safety.

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.380
This is the video for module one of Basic Baking and Snacks Production.

3
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:25.740
At any school gate or motor park in Nigeria, you see the same snacks: meat pie, chin chin, puff-puff, bread and cake. Chin chin and puff-puff are fried in hot oil. They need only a stove and a deep pot, so they are the cheapest way to start.

4
00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:34.240
Bread, cake and meat pie are baked in an oven. They need more equipment, but usually sell at a higher price per piece.

5
00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:45.070
Shelf life is how long a product stays safe and pleasant to eat. Puff-puff and meat pie spoil fast. You cook in the morning and sell by afternoon.

6
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.720
So you need a steady customer group close by, like a school, an office or a market lane.

7
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:59.840
Well-made chin chin lasts. You can make it once or twice a week, seal it in bags, and sell slowly through shops.

8
00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:13.950
For example, Bisi in Ibadan has a gas stove and no oven. She fries puff-puff each school morning for the break, and makes chin chin on Saturdays. Choose a product that fits the hours you really have.

9
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:28.430
A recipe is not magic. Flour and water form gluten, a stretchy net. Kneading makes it strong, so bread traps gas and rises. For pastry and cake, mix gently, so the bite stays tender.

10
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:37.310
Yeast is a living fungus. It eats sugar and gives off gas, but it needs warmth and time. Very hot water kills it.

11
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:47.160
Baking powder releases gas when wet and again when heated. Baking soda only works with an acid, like yoghurt or lemon juice.

12
00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:54.660
Fat coats the flour and shortens gluten. That is why pastry with plenty of cold fat is flaky.

13
00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:03.800
Sugar sweetens, feeds yeast and browns the crust. Eggs bind and set firm. Salt adds flavour and controls yeast.

14
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:17.070
For example, Amaka's puff-puff batter did not rise, and the balls fried flat and heavy. First she checks the yeast. The sachet was opened two months ago and left in a hot kitchen.

15
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.520
Then the water. She used water just off the boil, because the morning was cold. That killed the yeast.

16
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:30.530
Next batch, she uses a fresh sachet and water that feels lukewarm on her wrist.

17
00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:40.590
She covers the bowl in a warm spot. In about 45 minutes it has doubled and is full of bubbles, and the puff-puff comes out light and round.

18
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:48.180
When you sell food, customers trust you with their health. Follow the World Health Organization's Five Keys.

19
00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:59.710
Keep clean. Wash hands with soap before handling food, and after the toilet, raw meat, money or your phone. Separate raw and cooked. Use a separate board and knife for raw meat.

20
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:07.270
Cook thoroughly. Cook meat filling on the stove until no pink remains, at least 70 degrees Celsius at the centre.

21
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:18.430
Keep food out of the danger zone, between 5 and 60 degrees, and never more than 2 hours at room temperature. And use safe water, good flour and uncracked eggs.

22
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:27.280
Never leave hot oil unattended, and keep children away from the stove. Lower food into the oil gently, away from you.

23
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:38.260
If oil catches fire, turn off the gas if you can reach it safely, and cover the pot with a lid or fire blanket. Never pour water on an oil fire.

24
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:45.910
Keep the gas cylinder upright and ventilated, and check hoses for leaks with soapy water, never a flame.

25
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:56.500
So remember. Fried snacks need only a stove. Short shelf life means make close to the time of sale. Yeast needs lukewarm water and time.

26
00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:02.720
Follow the Five Keys. And the most important tool for repeatable baking is a digital scale.

27
00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:13.990
Your practice: map five snacks sold near you, write an ingredient job sheet, set up a clean workstation with a handwashing point, and check your gas hose.

28
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:16.500
Now continue with the lessons below.
